B (primary), LLaMA-3.1-8B-Instruct (cross-model)\n- LoRA fine-tuning, XGrammar constrained decoding\n- Default: N=8, T=0.7; varies N∈{2,4,8,16}, T∈{0.7,1.0}\n\n## Results (10 tables across 9 pages):\n**Table 1 (Cross-dataset NER):** SciERC ρ_SJ=.448, CoNLL ρ_SJ=.412, OntoNotes ρ_SJ=.395. All ρ_SJ > ρ_FK with p < 10^-27. SciERC uses N=16, others N=8.\n**Table 2 (N-scaling):** N=8→16 gives ρ_SJ +0.065 vs ρ_FK +0.030.\n**Table 3 (Cross-model):** LLaMA ρ_SJ=.368 (vs Qwen .383), delta=+.083.\n**Table 4 (OOD):** ρ_SJ degrades from .395 to .342 (-13.4%) under domain shift.\n**Table 5 (Temperature):** NER T=0.7 ρ=.388 vs T=1.0 ρ=.383. RE conditional ρ jumps from .316 to .372 at T=0.7.\n**Table 6 (NER vs RE N-scaling):** NER ρ_SJ gains +0.065 at N=16; RE gains only +0.024.\n**Table 7 (Per-relation-type):** Used-for ρ=.36–.40 (high freq), Conjunction ρ=.08–.12 (low freq). r=0.72 with frequency, r=-0.65 with span distance.\n**Table 8 (Capability stratification):** Inverted-U: NER peaks at 0.6-0.9 bucket (ρ=.52), RE peaks at same bucket (ρ=.42). RE F1=0 instances: 28.2% vs NER 4.5%.\n**Table 9 (Best-of-N):** SJ F1=.706 (+3.9 over greedy), FK=.693 (+2.6), Random=.682 (+1.5), Oracle=.812.\n**Table 10 (Selection curves):** SJ consistently > FK > Random across N=1–16, gap widens with N.\n\n## Planned Experiment List (from experiment design doc)\n11 experiments were planned:\n1. NER N=16 on SciERC (N-scaling curve)\n2. NER on CoNLL-2003\n3. NER on OntoNotes\n4. RE diagnostic per-relation-type breakdown\n5. Temperature sensitivity T=0.7 vs T=1.0\n6. Adaptive compute best-of-N demo\n7. Cross-model (LLaMA-3.1-8B)\n8. RE N=16 confirmation\n9. Capability stratification by F1 buckets\n10. Selection curves N=1,2,4,8,16\n11. OOD robustness (OntoNotes cross-domain)\n\nAll 11 experiments appear to be covered in the paper.\n\n## Your Review Task (R1: Methods + Experiments)\n\nEvaluate:\n1. **Methodological soundness:** Is the soft Jaccard formulation well-motivated and technically correct? Is the evaluation protocol (Spearman ρ between confidence and greedy F1) appropriate? Any methodological gaps?\n2. **Experimental rigor:** Are the experiments well-designed? Statistical significance? Are there confounds? Do results support claims?\n3. **Experimental coverage:** Are the experiments sufficient to support the claims? Any missing ablations or baselines?\n4. **Reproducibility concerns:** Are details sufficient to reproduce?\n5. **Weaknesses in methods/experiments**\n\nUse a 6-point scale: 1=Strong Reject, 2=Reject, 3=Weak Reject, 4=Weak Accept, 5=Accept, 6=Strong Accept.\n\nProvide:\n- Summary (2-3 sentences)\n- Strengths (bulleted)\n- Weaknesses (bulleted, with severity: minor/moderate/major)\n- Questions for authors\n- Score and confidence (1-5)\n- Missing experiments from the planned list (if any)\n","uuid":"d21309ca-d735-4b3a-b467-dcda9d2bf247","session_id":"2168b128-bc29-48d5-839e-470b564c8edf"}
{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"type":"text","text":"You are Reviewer R1 in a DREAM review roundtable. Your focus is **Methods and Experiments**. Review the following paper and provide an independent, detailed review.\n\n# Paper: \"Structural Self-Consistency Enables Test-Time Scaling for Entity-Level Information Extraction\"\n\n## Paper Summary\nThe paper proposes using soft Jaccard similarity over structured IE outputs as a \"structural self-consistency\" confidence signal for test-time scaling (TTS). The idea: sample N outputs from an LLM, compute pairwise soft Jaccard across samples, and use mean SJ as a confidence score to select the best sample (best-of-N).\n\n## Key Claims\n- **Claim 1 (Positive):** For NER, soft Jaccard achieves Spearman ρ=0.40–0.45 with instance-level F1, outperforming Fleiss' Kappa (ρ=0.29–0.32). Translates to +3.9 F1 via best-of-N at N=16.\n- **Claim 2 (Diagnostic):** For RE, full-set ρ drops to 0.27 due to compound error propagation, but conditional analysis (excluding knowledge-gap instances) recovers ρ=0.40.\n\n## Method Details\n- **Soft Jaccard (SJ):** For two sampled outputs y_a, y_b (sets of entities), SJ = sum of best-match scores / total elements. NER match: span Jaccard × type indicator. RE match: head_match × tail_match × relation_type indicator.\n- **Confidence score:** Mean pairwise SJ across C(N,2) pairs.\n- **Baseline:** Fleiss' Kappa over BIO token labels.\n- **Evaluation:** Spearman ρ between confidence score and instance-level F1 of greedy prediction. Full ρ (all instances) and conditional ρ (excluding all-zero instances).\n- **Best-of-N:** Select sample with highest leave-one-out mean SJ.\n\n## Experimental Setup\n- 3 NER datasets: SciERC (529 test, 6 types), CoNLL-2003 (3453 test, 4 types), OntoNotes (8262 test, 18 types)\n- RE: SciERC 7 relation types\n- Models: Qwen3-8B (primary), LLaMA-3.1-8B-Instruct (cross-model)\n- LoRA fine-tuning, XGrammar constrained decoding\n- Default: N=8, T=0.7; varies N∈{2,4,8,16}, T∈{0.7,1.0}\n\n## Results (10 tables across 9 pages):\n**Table 1 (Cross-dataset NER):** SciERC ρ_SJ=.448, CoNLL ρ_SJ=.412, OntoNotes ρ_SJ=.395. All ρ_SJ > ρ_FK with p < 10^-27. SciERC uses N=16, others N=8.\n**Table 2 (N-scaling):** N=8→16 gives ρ_SJ +0.065 vs ρ_FK +0.030.\n**Table 3 (Cross-model):** LLaMA ρ_SJ=.368 (vs Qwen .383), delta=+.083.\n**Table 4 (OOD):** ρ_SJ degrades from .395 to .342 (-13.4%) under domain shift.\n**Table 5 (Temperature):** NER T=0.7 ρ=.388 vs T=1.0 ρ=.383. RE conditional ρ jumps from .316 to .372 at T=0.7.\n**Table 6 (NER vs RE N-scaling):** NER ρ_SJ gains +0.065 at N=16; RE gains only +0.024.\n**Table 7 (Per-relation-type):** Used-for ρ=.36–.40 (high freq), Conjunction ρ=.08–.12 (low freq). r=0.72 with frequency, r=-0.65 with span distance.\n**Table 8 (Capability stratification):** Inverted-U: NER peaks at 0.6-0.9 bucket (ρ=.52), RE peaks at same bucket (ρ=.42). RE F1=0 instances: 28.2% vs NER 4.5%.\n**Table 9 (Best-of-N):** SJ F1=.706 (+3.9 over greedy), FK=.693 (+2.6), Random=.682 (+1.5), Oracle=.812.\n**Table 10 (Selection curves):** SJ consistently > FK > Random across N=1–16, gap widens with N.\n\n## Planned Experiment List (from experiment design doc)\n11 experiments were planned:\n1. NER N=16 on SciERC (N-scaling curve)\n2. NER on CoNLL-2003\n3. NER on OntoNotes\n4. RE diagnostic per-relation-type breakdown\n5. Temperature sensitivity T=0.7 vs T=1.0\n6. Adaptive compute best-of-N demo\n7. Cross-model (LLaMA-3.1-8B)\n8. RE N=16 confirmation\n9. Capability stratification by F1 buckets\n10. Selection curves N=1,2,4,8,16\n11. OOD robustness (OntoNotes cross-domain)\n\nAll 11 experiments appear to be covered in the paper.\n\n## Your Review Task (R1: Methods + Experiments)\n\nEvaluate:\n1. **Methodological soundness:** Is the soft Jaccard formulation well-motivated and technically correct? Is the evaluation protocol (Spearman ρ between confidence and greedy F1) appropriate? Any methodological gaps?\n2. **Experimental rigor:** Are the experiments well-designed? Statistical significance? Are there confounds? Do results support claims?\n3. **Experimental coverage:** Are the experiments sufficient to support the claims? Any missing ablations or baselines?\n4. **Reproducibility concerns:** Are details sufficient to reproduce?\n5. **Weaknesses in methods/experiments**\n\nUse a 6-point scale: 1=Strong Reject, 2=Reject, 3=Weak Reject, 4=Weak Accept, 5=Accept, 6=Strong Accept.\n\nProvide:\n- Summary (2-3 sentences)\n- Strengths (bulleted)\n- Weaknesses (bulleted, with severity: minor/moderate/major)\n- Questions for authors\n- Score and confidence (1-5)\n- Missing experiments from the planned list (if any)\n"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":"toolu_0171FYb22o7vwarcEdYKpY5W","session_id":"2168b128-bc29-48d5-839e-470b564c8edf","uuid":"08db5ddf-d31b-4afe-8598-464f5ef3ff54","timestamp":"2026-05-14T10:08:41.208Z"}
