{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_0171FYb22o7vwarcEdYKpY5W","type":"tool_result","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Now I have all the information needed. Let me compile the review.\n\n---\n\n## R1 Review: Methods and Experiments\n\n### Summary\n\nThis paper proposes soft Jaccard similarity over structured IE outputs as a \"structural self-consistency\" confidence signal for test-time scaling. The core finding is that for NER, soft Jaccard achieves rho=0.40-0.45 Spearman correlation with instance-level F1 (vs rho=0.29-0.32 for Fleiss' Kappa), translating to +3.9 F1 via best-of-N selection. For RE, full-set correlation degrades to rho=0.27 due to compound error propagation, but conditional analysis recovers rho=0.40. The paper presents 10 tables across 11 planned experiments covering cross-dataset, cross-model, temperature, and capability stratification analyses.\n\n### Strengths\n\n- **Well-motivated metric design.** Soft Jaccard respects the set-based structure of IE outputs, and the paper clearly articulates why surface-level metrics (Fleiss' Kappa on BIO labels) lose information about structural similarity. The formulation is technically clean: character-level span Jaccard multiplied by type indicator for NER, extended multiplicatively for RE head/tail/type.\n\n- **Comprehensive experimental coverage.** All 11 planned experiments are covered. The paper systematically varies dataset (3 NER benchmarks spanning scientific/news/mixed domains with 4-18 entity types), model family (Qwen3-8B and LLaMA-3.1-8B), sample budget (N=2 through 16), temperature (T=0.7 vs 1.0), and distribution shift (in-domain vs OOD). This is thorough for a primarily analytical paper.\n\n- **Strong statistical significance.** All SJ vs FK comparisons for NER report p < 10^{-27}, and the large test sets (CoNLL 3,453; OntoNotes 8,262) provide high statistical power. The SJ > FK finding is consistent across every NER experimental condition.\n\n- **Honest treatment of negative results.** The RE analysis is commendably transparent. Rather than hiding the failure, the paper provides per-relation-type decomposition (Table 7), capability stratification (Table 8), and temperature analysis (Table 5) that together construct a mechanistic explanation for when and why structural consistency fails. The inverted-U pattern in capability stratification is a genuine insight.\n\n- **Practical validation.** The best-of-N selection experiment (Table 9) converts correlation into concrete F1 gains, and the selection curves (Table 10) show monotonic SJ advantage across all N values. This addresses the \"so what?\" question effectively.\n\n### Weaknesses\n\n- **(Major) Code-paper discrepancy in SJ formula.** The paper defines SJ (Equation 1) as a symmetric max-match formulation: sum of best-match scores from both directions divided by |y_a| + |y_b|. However, the implementation uses Hungarian (optimal) matching within type groups with `max(|ga|, |gb|)` as denominator per group, then computes a weighted average across groups. These are different algorithms. The paper formula permits many-to-one matching (each element takes its best match independently), while the code enforces one-to-one matching via `linear_sum_assignment`. Moreover, the paper does not mention grouping by entity type before matching, which is a significant implementation detail. The denominator also differs: paper uses |y_a|+|y_b| (sum), code uses max(|ga|, |gb|) per type group. Readers attempting to reproduce would implement the paper's formula and get different results.\n\n- **(Major) Evaluation protocol circularity concern.** The confidence score c(x) is correlated against the greedy (T=0) prediction's F1, but the best-of-N selection operates on sampled (T=0.7) outputs. The paper does not clearly justify why correlation with greedy F1 is the right proxy for selection quality among stochastic samples. The implicit assumption is that greedy F1 and sample quality are monotonically related at the instance level, but this is not verified. A more direct evaluation would correlate the SJ score of each sample with that sample's own F1.\n\n- **(Major) Inconsistent N across Table 1.** SciERC uses N=16 while CoNLL and OntoNotes use N=8. This makes direct cross-dataset comparison of rho values confounded by a known factor (N-scaling effect). Table 2 shows N=8->16 gives +0.065 rho for SJ on SciERC. The SciERC rho_SJ=0.448 in Table 1 would be ~0.383 at N=8, which is actually the lowest among the three datasets, not the highest as Table 1 presents. This asymmetry should be addressed by either running all datasets at N=8 or all at N=16 for the main comparison table.\n\n- **(Moderate) Only one RE dataset (SciERC).** While three NER datasets are tested, the RE analysis relies entirely on SciERC, which has only 7 relation types and 397 test instances with relations. The diagnostic claims about compound error propagation are plausible but dataset-specific. A second RE dataset (e.g., TACRED, DocRED, or NYT) would substantially strengthen Claim 2. The per-relation-type analysis in Table 7 reports rho values as ranges (e.g., \"0.36-0.40\") rather than point estimates, which is unusual and reduces precision.\n\n- **(Moderate) No confidence intervals for rho.** While p-values for the SJ vs FK comparison are reported, bootstrap confidence intervals for individual rho values are absent. Given that rho~0.40 is a moderate correlation and some analyses stratify into small subsets (e.g., capability buckets with n=42-85), reporting CIs would help assess whether the rho differences across conditions are meaningful or within estimation noise.\n\n- **(Moderate) Limited baseline comparison.** Fleiss' Kappa is the only baseline consistency metric. Other natural competitors include: (a) mean log-probability of sampled outputs, (b) mutual information or BLEU/ROUGE-based agreement, (c) sequence-level exact match rate, (d) embedding-based similarity. The paper argues SJ is better than FK, but FK may be a strawman for NER where token-level BIO agreement ignores entity boundaries by construction.\n\n- **(Minor) Temperature sensitivity table inconsistency.** Table 5 reports NER T=0.7 headroom as 0.055 but Table 2 reports N=8 headroom as 0.108 (both on SciERC). If both are N=8, the discrepancy suggests different data or conditions. If Table 5's NER is N=8 at T=0.7 and Table 2 is N=8 at T=1.0, then the headroom values align with the temperature discussion, but the paper should make this explicit.\n\n- **(Minor) No RE best-of-N results.** The adaptive compute demo (Table 9) and selection curves (Table 10) are NER-only. Even a negative result showing SJ-based selection does not improve RE F1 would complete the diagnostic story.\n\n- **(Minor) OntoNotes licensing.** The experiment plan notes uncertainty about LDC license access. The paper reports OntoNotes 5.0 results without clarification of whether the actual dataset or a surrogate was used.\n\n### Questions for Authors\n\n1. Can you reconcile the SJ formula in Equation 1 (symmetric max-match, denominator |y_a|+|y_b|) with the implementation (Hungarian matching within type groups, denominator max(|ga|,|gb|) per group)? Which version produced the reported results?\n\n2. Why is the confidence score correlated against greedy F1 rather than the individual sample's F1? Have you verified that greedy F1 is a good proxy for sample-level quality ranking?\n\n3. For Table 1, can you provide SciERC NER results at N=8 alongside CoNLL and OntoNotes N=8 for an apples-to-apples comparison?\n\n4. The per-relation-type rho values in Table 7 are reported as ranges rather than point estimates. What explains these ranges -- are they across multiple seeds, bootstrap intervals, or different experimental conditions?\n\n5. How does the SJ computation handle duplicate entities within a single sample (same span and type predicted multiple times)?\n\n6. Have you considered model-internal confidence baselines (e.g., mean token-level log-probability) as an alternative to self-consistency?\n\n### Score\n\n**4 (Weak Accept)**\n\nThe paper addresses a relevant problem (TTS for structured prediction), proposes a sensible metric, and provides thorough experiments across multiple dimensions. The diagnostic analysis of why RE fails is genuinely insightful. However, the code-paper formula discrepancy is concerning for reproducibility, the inconsistent N across the main comparison table muddies the headline result, and the baseline comparison is narrow. The core contribution -- showing that soft Jaccard outperforms Fleiss' Kappa as a self-consistency metric -- is sound but incremental; the effect size (rho improvement of 0.08-0.15 over FK) is moderate, and the practical gain (+3.9 F1 capturing 27% of oracle headroom) leaves substantial room for improvement.\n\n### Confidence\n\n**4 out of 5.** I have carefully reviewed the paper, the codebase implementation, the experiment plan, and the development history. The methodology and experimental design are within my area of expertise. My main uncertainty is around whether the code-paper discrepancy materially affects the results (it may not change the qualitative conclusions, but it affects reproducibility assessment).\n\n### Missing Experiments from Planned List\n\nAll 11 planned experiments appear to be covered in the paper. No planned experiments are missing."},{"type":"text","text":"agentId: afec1f1dffcc66641 (use SendMessage with to: 'afec1f1dffcc66641' to continue this agent)\n<usage>total_tokens: 85419\ntool_uses: 21\nduration_ms: 127881</usage>"}]}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"2168b128-bc29-48d5-839e-470b564c8edf","uuid":"511f2464-083c-4d26-aeeb-f3e74e52c76c","timestamp":"2026-05-14T10:10:49.090Z","tool_use_result":{"status":"completed","prompt":"You are Reviewer R1 in a DREAM review roundtable. Your focus is **Methods and Experiments**. Review the following paper and provide an independent, detailed review.\n\n# Paper: \"Structural Self-Consistency Enables Test-Time Scaling for Entity-Level Information Extraction\"\n\n## Paper Summary\nThe paper proposes using soft Jaccard similarity over structured IE outputs as a \"structural self-consistency\" confidence signal for test-time scaling (TTS). The idea: sample N outputs from an LLM, compute pairwise soft Jaccard across samples, and use mean SJ as a confidence score to select the best sample (best-of-N).\n\n## Key Claims\n- **Claim 1 (Positive):** For NER, soft Jaccard achieves Spearman ρ=0.40–0.45 with instance-level F1, outperforming Fleiss' Kappa (ρ=0.29–0.32). Translates to +3.9 F1 via best-of-N at N=16.\n- **Claim 2 (Diagnostic):** For RE, full-set ρ drops to 0.27 due to compound error propagation, but conditional analysis (excluding knowledge-gap instances) recovers ρ=0.40.\n\n## Method Details\n- **Soft Jaccard (SJ):** For two sampled outputs y_a, y_b (sets of entities), SJ = sum of best-match scores / total elements. NER match: span Jaccard × type indicator. RE match: head_match × tail_match × relation_type indicator.\n- **Confidence score:** Mean pairwise SJ across C(N,2) pairs.\n- **Baseline:** Fleiss' Kappa over BIO token labels.\n- **Evaluation:** Spearman ρ between confidence score and instance-level F1 of greedy prediction. Full ρ (all instances) and conditional ρ (excluding all-zero instances).\n- **Best-of-N:** Select sample with highest leave-one-out mean SJ.\n\n## Experimental Setup\n- 3 NER datasets: SciERC (529 test, 6 types), CoNLL-2003 (3453 test, 4 types), OntoNotes (8262 test, 18 types)\n- RE: SciERC 7 relation types\n- Models: Qwen3-8B (primary), LLaMA-3.1-8B-Instruct (cross-model)\n- LoRA fine-tuning, XGrammar constrained decoding\n- Default: N=8, T=0.7; varies N∈{2,4,8,16}, T∈{0.7,1.0}\n\n## Results (10 tables across 9 pages):\n**Table 1 (Cross-dataset NER):** SciERC ρ_SJ=.448, CoNLL ρ_SJ=.412, OntoNotes ρ_SJ=.395. All ρ_SJ > ρ_FK with p < 10^-27. SciERC uses N=16, others N=8.\n**Table 2 (N-scaling):** N=8→16 gives ρ_SJ +0.065 vs ρ_FK +0.030.\n**Table 3 (Cross-model):** LLaMA ρ_SJ=.368 (vs Qwen .383), delta=+.083.\n**Table 4 (OOD):** ρ_SJ degrades from .395 to .342 (-13.4%) under domain shift.\n**Table 5 (Temperature):** NER T=0.7 ρ=.388 vs T=1.0 ρ=.383. RE conditional ρ jumps from .316 to .372 at T=0.7.\n**Table 6 (NER vs RE N-scaling):** NER ρ_SJ gains +0.065 at N=16; RE gains only +0.024.\n**Table 7 (Per-relation-type):** Used-for ρ=.36–.40 (high freq), Conjunction ρ=.08–.12 (low freq). r=0.72 with frequency, r=-0.65 with span distance.\n**Table 8 (Capability stratification):** Inverted-U: NER peaks at 0.6-0.9 bucket (ρ=.52), RE peaks at same bucket (ρ=.42). RE F1=0 instances: 28.2% vs NER 4.5%.\n**Table 9 (Best-of-N):** SJ F1=.706 (+3.9 over greedy), FK=.693 (+2.6), Random=.682 (+1.5), Oracle=.812.\n**Table 10 (Selection curves):** SJ consistently > FK > Random across N=1–16, gap widens with N.\n\n## Planned Experiment List (from experiment design doc)\n11 experiments were planned:\n1. NER N=16 on SciERC (N-scaling curve)\n2. NER on CoNLL-2003\n3. NER on OntoNotes\n4. RE diagnostic per-relation-type breakdown\n5. Temperature sensitivity T=0.7 vs T=1.0\n6. Adaptive compute best-of-N demo\n7. Cross-model (LLaMA-3.1-8B)\n8. RE N=16 confirmation\n9. Capability stratification by F1 buckets\n10. Selection curves N=1,2,4,8,16\n11. OOD robustness (OntoNotes cross-domain)\n\nAll 11 experiments appear to be covered in the paper.\n\n## Your Review Task (R1: Methods + Experiments)\n\nEvaluate:\n1. **Methodological soundness:** Is the soft Jaccard formulation well-motivated and technically correct? Is the evaluation protocol (Spearman ρ between confidence and greedy F1) appropriate? Any methodological gaps?\n2. **Experimental rigor:** Are the experiments well-designed? Statistical significance? Are there confounds? Do results support claims?\n3. **Experimental coverage:** Are the experiments sufficient to support the claims? Any missing ablations or baselines?\n4. **Reproducibility concerns:** Are details sufficient to reproduce?\n5. **Weaknesses in methods/experiments**\n\nUse a 6-point scale: 1=Strong Reject, 2=Reject, 3=Weak Reject, 4=Weak Accept, 5=Accept, 6=Strong Accept.\n\nProvide:\n- Summary (2-3 sentences)\n- Strengths (bulleted)\n- Weaknesses (bulleted, with severity: minor/moderate/major)\n- Questions for authors\n- Score and confidence (1-5)\n- Missing experiments from the planned list (if any)\n","agentId":"afec1f1dffcc66641","agentType":"general-purpose","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Now I have all the information needed. Let me compile the review.\n\n---\n\n## R1 Review: Methods and Experiments\n\n### Summary\n\nThis paper proposes soft Jaccard similarity over structured IE outputs as a \"structural self-consistency\" confidence signal for test-time scaling. The core finding is that for NER, soft Jaccard achieves rho=0.40-0.45 Spearman correlation with instance-level F1 (vs rho=0.29-0.32 for Fleiss' Kappa), translating to +3.9 F1 via best-of-N selection. For RE, full-set correlation degrades to rho=0.27 due to compound error propagation, but conditional analysis recovers rho=0.40. The paper presents 10 tables across 11 planned experiments covering cross-dataset, cross-model, temperature, and capability stratification analyses.\n\n### Strengths\n\n- **Well-motivated metric design.** Soft Jaccard respects the set-based structure of IE outputs, and the paper clearly articulates why surface-level metrics (Fleiss' Kappa on BIO labels) lose information about structural similarity. The formulation is technically clean: character-level span Jaccard multiplied by type indicator for NER, extended multiplicatively for RE head/tail/type.\n\n- **Comprehensive experimental coverage.** All 11 planned experiments are covered. The paper systematically varies dataset (3 NER benchmarks spanning scientific/news/mixed domains with 4-18 entity types), model family (Qwen3-8B and LLaMA-3.1-8B), sample budget (N=2 through 16), temperature (T=0.7 vs 1.0), and distribution shift (in-domain vs OOD). This is thorough for a primarily analytical paper.\n\n- **Strong statistical significance.** All SJ vs FK comparisons for NER report p < 10^{-27}, and the large test sets (CoNLL 3,453; OntoNotes 8,262) provide high statistical power. The SJ > FK finding is consistent across every NER experimental condition.\n\n- **Honest treatment of negative results.** The RE analysis is commendably transparent. Rather than hiding the failure, the paper provides per-relation-type decomposition (Table 7), capability stratification (Table 8), and temperature analysis (Table 5) that together construct a mechanistic explanation for when and why structural consistency fails. The inverted-U pattern in capability stratification is a genuine insight.\n\n- **Practical validation.** The best-of-N selection experiment (Table 9) converts correlation into concrete F1 gains, and the selection curves (Table 10) show monotonic SJ advantage across all N values. This addresses the \"so what?\" question effectively.\n\n### Weaknesses\n\n- **(Major) Code-paper discrepancy in SJ formula.** The paper defines SJ (Equation 1) as a symmetric max-match formulation: sum of best-match scores from both directions divided by |y_a| + |y_b|. However, the implementation uses Hungarian (optimal) matching within type groups with `max(|ga|, |gb|)` as denominator per group, then computes a weighted average across groups. These are different algorithms. The paper formula permits many-to-one matching (each element takes its best match independently), while the code enforces one-to-one matching via `linear_sum_assignment`. Moreover, the paper does not mention grouping by entity type before matching, which is a significant implementation detail. The denominator also differs: paper uses |y_a|+|y_b| (sum), code uses max(|ga|, |gb|) per type group. Readers attempting to reproduce would implement the paper's formula and get different results.\n\n- **(Major) Evaluation protocol circularity concern.** The confidence score c(x) is correlated against the greedy (T=0) prediction's F1, but the best-of-N selection operates on sampled (T=0.7) outputs. The paper does not clearly justify why correlation with greedy F1 is the right proxy for selection quality among stochastic samples. The implicit assumption is that greedy F1 and sample quality are monotonically related at the instance level, but this is not verified. A more direct evaluation would correlate the SJ score of each sample with that sample's own F1.\n\n- **(Major) Inconsistent N across Table 1.** SciERC uses N=16 while CoNLL and OntoNotes use N=8. This makes direct cross-dataset comparison of rho values confounded by a known factor (N-scaling effect). Table 2 shows N=8->16 gives +0.065 rho for SJ on SciERC. The SciERC rho_SJ=0.448 in Table 1 would be ~0.383 at N=8, which is actually the lowest among the three datasets, not the highest as Table 1 presents. This asymmetry should be addressed by either running all datasets at N=8 or all at N=16 for the main comparison table.\n\n- **(Moderate) Only one RE dataset (SciERC).** While three NER datasets are tested, the RE analysis relies entirely on SciERC, which has only 7 relation types and 397 test instances with relations. The diagnostic claims about compound error propagation are plausible but dataset-specific. A second RE dataset (e.g., TACRED, DocRED, or NYT) would substantially strengthen Claim 2. The per-relation-type analysis in Table 7 reports rho values as ranges (e.g., \"0.36-0.40\") rather than point estimates, which is unusual and reduces precision.\n\n- **(Moderate) No confidence intervals for rho.** While p-values for the SJ vs FK comparison are reported, bootstrap confidence intervals for individual rho values are absent. Given that rho~0.40 is a moderate correlation and some analyses stratify into small subsets (e.g., capability buckets with n=42-85), reporting CIs would help assess whether the rho differences across conditions are meaningful or within estimation noise.\n\n- **(Moderate) Limited baseline comparison.** Fleiss' Kappa is the only baseline consistency metric. Other natural competitors include: (a) mean log-probability of sampled outputs, (b) mutual information or BLEU/ROUGE-based agreement, (c) sequence-level exact match rate, (d) embedding-based similarity. The paper argues SJ is better than FK, but FK may be a strawman for NER where token-level BIO agreement ignores entity boundaries by construction.\n\n- **(Minor) Temperature sensitivity table inconsistency.** Table 5 reports NER T=0.7 headroom as 0.055 but Table 2 reports N=8 headroom as 0.108 (both on SciERC). If both are N=8, the discrepancy suggests different data or conditions. If Table 5's NER is N=8 at T=0.7 and Table 2 is N=8 at T=1.0, then the headroom values align with the temperature discussion, but the paper should make this explicit.\n\n- **(Minor) No RE best-of-N results.** The adaptive compute demo (Table 9) and selection curves (Table 10) are NER-only. Even a negative result showing SJ-based selection does not improve RE F1 would complete the diagnostic story.\n\n- **(Minor) OntoNotes licensing.** The experiment plan notes uncertainty about LDC license access. The paper reports OntoNotes 5.0 results without clarification of whether the actual dataset or a surrogate was used.\n\n### Questions for Authors\n\n1. Can you reconcile the SJ formula in Equation 1 (symmetric max-match, denominator |y_a|+|y_b|) with the implementation (Hungarian matching within type groups, denominator max(|ga|,|gb|) per group)? Which version produced the reported results?\n\n2. Why is the confidence score correlated against greedy F1 rather than the individual sample's F1? Have you verified that greedy F1 is a good proxy for sample-level quality ranking?\n\n3. For Table 1, can you provide SciERC NER results at N=8 alongside CoNLL and OntoNotes N=8 for an apples-to-apples comparison?\n\n4. The per-relation-type rho values in Table 7 are reported as ranges rather than point estimates. What explains these ranges -- are they across multiple seeds, bootstrap intervals, or different experimental conditions?\n\n5. How does the SJ computation handle duplicate entities within a single sample (same span and type predicted multiple times)?\n\n6. Have you considered model-internal confidence baselines (e.g., mean token-level log-probability) as an alternative to self-consistency?\n\n### Score\n\n**4 (Weak Accept)**\n\nThe paper addresses a relevant problem (TTS for structured prediction), proposes a sensible metric, and provides thorough experiments across multiple dimensions. The diagnostic analysis of why RE fails is genuinely insightful. However, the code-paper formula discrepancy is concerning for reproducibility, the inconsistent N across the main comparison table muddies the headline result, and the baseline comparison is narrow. The core contribution -- showing that soft Jaccard outperforms Fleiss' Kappa as a self-consistency metric -- is sound but incremental; the effect size (rho improvement of 0.08-0.15 over FK) is moderate, and the practical gain (+3.9 F1 capturing 27% of oracle headroom) leaves substantial room for improvement.\n\n### Confidence\n\n**4 out of 5.** I have carefully reviewed the paper, the codebase implementation, the experiment plan, and the development history. The methodology and experimental design are within my area of expertise. My main uncertainty is around whether the code-paper discrepancy materially affects the results (it may not change the qualitative conclusions, but it affects reproducibility assessment).\n\n### Missing Experiments from Planned List\n\nAll 11 planned experiments appear to be covered in the paper. No planned experiments are missing."}],"totalDurationMs":127881,"totalTokens":85419,"totalToolUseCount":21,"usage":{"input_tokens":1,"cache_creation_input_tokens":972,"cache_read_input_tokens":82111,"output_tokens":2335,"server_tool_use":{"web_search_