{"type":"system","subtype":"task_progress","task_id":"ae7321839ca81c909","tool_use_id":"toolu_017ZTLCwVmMYjZrtaSRhdD2q","description":"R2: Novelty + Related Work review","usage":{"total_tokens":43604,"tool_uses":18,"duration_ms":130382},"last_tool_name":"mcp__agent-ml-research__submit_dream_review_report","uuid":"2264a9b6-ab57-4f9c-8cf0-6c1cf66e36e2","session_id":"2168b128-bc29-48d5-839e-470b564c8edf"}
{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-4-6","id":"msg_01YPxtAG1BmWSEC1KEYwoKht","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_017xUTmcGKmG8T9NyJaM8KVH","name":"mcp__agent-ml-research__submit_dream_review_report","input":{"report":"\n# R2 Review: Novelty and Related Work\n\n## Paper: \"Structural Self-Consistency Enables Test-Time Scaling for Entity-Level Information Extraction\"\n\n---\n\n## Summary\n\nThe paper proposes soft Jaccard similarity over structured IE outputs as a \"structural self-consistency\" confidence signal for test-time scaling, comparing it against Fleiss' Kappa on NER (3 datasets, 2 models) and RE (1 dataset). The core positive finding is a moderate correlation advantage for NER (rho ~0.40-0.45 vs ~0.29-0.32 for FK), translating to +3.9 F1 via best-of-16 selection. The diagnostic finding is that RE correlation collapses (rho=0.27 full-set) due to compound error propagation but recovers conditionally (rho=0.40).\n\n---\n\n## Strengths\n\n- **Reasonable research question.** Adapting self-consistency to structured prediction is a natural and timely question as LLM-based IE becomes mainstream. The observation that token-level agreement metrics discard structural information is valid and worth investigating.\n\n- **Diagnostic depth on RE failure.** The component-wise decomposition (E1), temperature analysis (T=0.7 as capability filter), and capability stratification provide genuine mechanistic insight into why consistency-based confidence fails for compound structures. This is the paper's strongest intellectual contribution.\n\n- **Thorough experimental controls.** Cross-dataset (SciERC, CoNLL-2003, OntoNotes), cross-model (Qwen3-8B, LLaMA-3.1-8B), N-scaling (N=8 to N=16), OOD evaluation, and temperature sensitivity together address many obvious confounds.\n\n- **Honest reporting of negative results.** The RE failure is not hidden; it is foregrounded and analyzed. The conditional analysis (distinguishing inability from uncertainty) is principled and well-motivated.\n\n---\n\n## Weaknesses\n\n### Major\n\n- **(Major) Limited novelty — this is a straightforward combination of known components.** Soft Jaccard / Dice / IoU over sets is a standard evaluation metric in IE and segmentation. Self-consistency via repeated sampling is established (Wang et al. 2023). The contribution reduces to: \"use an existing evaluation metric (soft Jaccard) as a self-consistency scoring function instead of another existing metric (Fleiss' Kappa).\" The \"structural\" framing overstates what is essentially a metric substitution. There is no new algorithm, no new theoretical result, no new training procedure. The paper reads as an empirical finding — that set-overlap metrics correlate better with accuracy than token-agreement metrics when comparing structured outputs — which, while useful, is somewhat expected given that set-overlap metrics are designed precisely to capture partial structural matches.\n\n- **(Major) Missing critical related work — Universal Self-Consistency (USC, Chen et al., 2024).** USC (NAACL 2024, \"Universal Self-Consistency for Large Language Model Generation\") directly addresses self-consistency for free-form / non-votable outputs by using an LLM to judge consistency between sampled responses. This is the most directly relevant prior work: it tackles the exact problem of extending self-consistency beyond majority voting to structured/complex outputs. The paper's claim that \"no systematic study exists on whether structural similarity between sampled outputs correlates with accuracy\" for structured prediction ignores USC. The paper should compare against an LLM-as-judge consistency baseline inspired by USC, or at minimum discuss why a handcrafted metric (soft Jaccard) is preferred over a learned/LLM-based consistency measure.\n\n- **(Major) Missing verifier/reward model baselines.** Test-time scaling literature increasingly uses trained verifiers (outcome reward models, process reward models) for sample selection. Snell et al. (2024), which the paper cites, explicitly compares verifier-based approaches against self-consistency. The paper ignores this entire class of approaches. Even a simple baseline — using the model's own log-probability as a confidence signal for best-of-N selection — is absent. Without comparing against likelihood-based or verifier-based selection, the paper cannot claim that consistency-based approaches are the right tool for this problem.\n\n### Moderate\n\n- **(Moderate) Narrow baseline comparison.** The only baseline is Fleiss' Kappa, which is a particularly weak strawman. FK operates at the token level (BIO labels) and is not designed for structured output comparison. More appropriate baselines include: (a) exact-match voting (majority vote on full output sets, the direct analogue of Wang et al.'s approach); (b) token-level F1 agreement between samples; (c) sequence log-probability; (d) embedding-based similarity between outputs. Beating FK does not establish that soft Jaccard is a strong confidence signal — it may just establish that FK is a poor one.\n\n- **(Moderate) Absolute correlation values are modest.** The best reported rho is 0.45 (NER, N=16). In calibration/confidence estimation literature, Spearman rho of 0.40-0.45 is generally considered moderate. The +3.9 F1 gain is meaningful but achieved on a relatively weak base model (greedy F1 = 0.667 on SciERC). On stronger systems (CoNLL greedy F1 ~0.89), the headroom shrinks to ~3.5pp and the practical value of consistency-based TTS diminishes.\n\n- **(Moderate) Missing structured prediction confidence estimation literature.** There is a body of work on confidence estimation for structured prediction (CRF-based NER, structured SVM, etc.) that predates LLM-based IE. Culotta & McCallum (2004, \"Confidence Estimation for Information Extraction\"), Nguyen & Guo (2007), and more recent work on calibration of sequence labeling models are relevant. The IE-specific confidence estimation space (attention entropy, softmax calibration) is mentioned in one sentence but not discussed.\n\n### Minor\n\n- **(Minor) Only 10 references is thin for an EMNLP submission.** A typical EMNLP paper has 30-50 references. The related work section appears to be a placeholder rather than a thorough literature survey.\n\n- **(Minor) \"Structural self-consistency\" terminology is somewhat misleading.** The method operates on flat sets of (span, type) tuples, not on structures with internal dependencies (trees, graphs). A dependency parse or AMR graph has genuine structure; a set of entity mentions is just a set. The term \"structural\" overpromises.\n\n---\n\n## Missing Related Work\n\n1. **Universal Self-Consistency (USC)** — Chen et al. (NAACL 2024). Extends self-consistency to free-form generation using LLM-as-judge for consistency evaluation. Directly competes with the paper's core idea.\n\n2. **Verifier/reward model approaches for TTS** — Cobbe et al. (2021) training verifiers for math; Lightman et al. (2023) process reward models; the verifier branch of Snell et al. (2024). These are the dominant TTS paradigm and must be discussed.\n\n3. **Sequence log-probability as confidence** — Kadavath et al. (2022, \"Language Models (Mostly) Know What They Know\"); Kuhn et al. (2023, \"Semantic Uncertainty\"); Xiong et al. (2024, \"Can LLMs Express Their Uncertainty?\"). Token-level and sequence-level probability as selection signals.\n\n4. **Semantic uncertainty / meaning-based clustering** — Kuhn et al. (2023) cluster semantically equivalent answers before computing consistency. This is analogous to what soft Jaccard does (partial credit for near-matches) but formalized differently.\n\n5. **Confidence estimation for IE** — Culotta & McCallum (2004, \"Confidence Estimation for Information Extraction\"); recent work on calibration of NER systems; Li et al. (2022) on uncertainty quantification for IE.\n\n6. **Other structural similarity metrics** — Tree edit distance, graph edit distance, graph matching algorithms. If the claim is \"structural\" consistency, why not compare against metrics that operate on richer structures (dependency-labeled entity graphs, AMR subgraphs)?\n\n7. **Multi-sample IE strategies** — Li et al. (2023, \"Coder: Knowledge-infused cross-lingual medical NER\") and other works that sample multiple IE outputs and aggregate. The general idea of sampling + aggregation for IE is not new.\n\n8. **Self-consistency variants** — Li et al. (2023, \"Making Language Models Better Reasoners with Step-Aware Verifier\"); diverse verifier approaches. The landscape of post-Wang (2023) self-consistency extensions is rich and barely touched.\n\n---\n\n## Questions for Authors\n\n1. How does soft Jaccard selection compare against simply using the mean sequence log-probability as a selection criterion for best-of-N? This is the most natural baseline and its absence is conspicuous.\n\n2. Have you considered an LLM-as-judge approach to consistency (a la USC)? For instance, asking the model to evaluate which of two sampled outputs is more likely correct, then using pairwise tournament ranking for selection?\n\n3. The \"structural\" framing implies more than set overlap. Have you experimented with metrics that capture relational structure — e.g., computing similarity over the entity-relation graph rather than flat entity/relation sets?\n\n4. The conditional analysis removes all-zero instances. What is the decision boundary? If we vary the threshold (e.g., remove instances where fewer than k of N samples produce non-empty output), how sensitive is the conditional rho?\n\n5. For the best-of-N result (+3.9 F1), what is the performance of a simpler heuristic: select the sample with the median output length (or median number of entities)? Length/count heuristics often work surprisingly well and cost nothing.\n\n---\n\n## Score: 3 (Weak Reject)\n\n## Confidence: 4/5\n\n---\n\n## Justification\n\nThe paper investigates a reasonable question — how to construct confidence signals for LLM-based IE test-time scaling — and provides thorough empirical analysis with honest treatment of negative results. However, the core contribution (using soft Jaccard instead of Fleiss' Kappa as a consistency metric) is an incremental metric substitution rather than a conceptual advance. The related work coverage has significant gaps: Universal Self-Consistency (the most directly relevant prior work), verifier-based TTS approaches, and sequence-probability baselines are all missing. The baseline comparison against only Fleiss' Kappa is too narrow to support the paper's claims. The absolute correlation values (rho ~0.40-0.45) are moderate, and the practical gains are concentrated on weaker base models where the headroom is large.\n\nFor an EMNLP Main acceptance, I would need to see: (1) comparison against log-probability and USC-style baselines, (2) a more complete related work section, and (3) either a stronger theoretical motivation for why soft Jaccard should work or substantially larger empirical gains. The diagnostic analysis of RE failure is the paper's strongest element and could be the seed of a more interesting paper about the relationship between task structure and self-consistency effectiveness — but it needs more development.\n\nThe paper is currently positioned between Findings and Reject. With stronger baselines and better contextualization against USC and verifier approaches, it could reach Findings level.\n","project":"struct_self_consist_ie","tier":"best","dream_id":"dream_20260514T093336","overall_score":"weak reject"},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":1,"cache_creation_input_tokens":513,"cache_read_input_tokens":42425,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":513,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"output_tokens":1,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":"toolu_017ZTLCwVmMYjZrtaSRhdD2q","session_id":"2168b128-bc29-48d5-839e-470b564c8edf","uuid":"eb338653-534b-4ad3-b887-c3aae07e812a"}
