{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_017ZTLCwVmMYjZrtaSRhdD2q","type":"tool_result","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Review submitted. Here is the summary of my R2 assessment:\n\n**Score: 3 (Weak Reject) | Confidence: 4/5**\n\nKey findings from the Novelty and Related Work perspective:\n\n**Core novelty concern:** The contribution reduces to substituting one existing metric (soft Jaccard) for another (Fleiss' Kappa) as a self-consistency scoring function. The \"structural\" framing overstates what is essentially a metric swap on flat sets of (span, type) tuples, not genuinely structured objects like trees or graphs.\n\n**Critical missing related work:**\n- Universal Self-Consistency (USC, Chen et al., NAACL 2024) -- directly addresses extending self-consistency to non-votable outputs via LLM-as-judge, the most relevant prior work\n- Verifier/reward model TTS approaches (Cobbe et al., Lightman et al.) -- the dominant TTS paradigm, completely absent\n- Sequence log-probability baselines (Kadavath et al., Kuhn et al.) -- the most natural competing selection signal\n- Semantic uncertainty clustering (Kuhn et al., 2023)\n- IE-specific confidence estimation (Culotta & McCallum 2004, etc.)\n\n**Baseline weakness:** Only comparing against Fleiss' Kappa (a weak strawman not designed for structured outputs) cannot establish that soft Jaccard is a strong confidence signal -- it may just confirm FK is a poor one. Missing: log-prob selection, exact-match voting, LLM-as-judge consistency.\n\n**Strongest element:** The RE diagnostic analysis (component decomposition, temperature as capability filter, capability stratification) provides genuine mechanistic insight and is the paper's most publishable contribution.\n\n**Path to acceptance:** Add log-probability and USC-style baselines, expand related work to 30+ references, and either provide theoretical grounding for why soft Jaccard works or demonstrate larger empirical gains. Currently between Findings and Reject."},{"type":"text","text":"agentId: ae7321839ca81c909 (use SendMessage with to: 'ae7321839ca81c909' to continue this agent)\n<usage>total_tokens: 46348\ntool_uses: 18\nduration_ms: 149971</usage>"}]}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"2168b128-bc29-48d5-839e-470b564c8edf","uuid":"92564596-4a4f-4a27-b012-792f6ed1233a","timestamp":"2026-05-14T10:11:32.136Z","tool_use_result":{"status":"completed","prompt":"You are Reviewer R2 in a DREAM review roundtable. Your focus is **Novelty and Related Work**. Review the following paper and provide an independent, detailed review.\n\n# Paper: \"Structural Self-Consistency Enables Test-Time Scaling for Entity-Level Information Extraction\"\n\n## Paper Summary\nThe paper proposes using soft Jaccard similarity over structured IE outputs as a \"structural self-consistency\" confidence signal for test-time scaling (TTS). Sample N outputs, compute mean pairwise soft Jaccard, use it to select the best sample (best-of-N). Tests on 3 NER datasets and 1 RE dataset.\n\n## Key Claims\n- **Claim 1:** For NER, soft Jaccard ρ=0.40–0.45 with F1, outperforming Fleiss' Kappa (ρ=0.29–0.32). +3.9 F1 via best-of-16.\n- **Claim 2:** RE full-set ρ=0.27 due to compound errors, but conditional ρ recovers to 0.40.\n\n## Method\n- **Soft Jaccard (SJ):** Pairwise set similarity with partial credit for span overlap. NER: span Jaccard × type indicator. RE: head_NER_match × tail_NER_match × relation_type indicator.\n- **Confidence:** Mean pairwise SJ over C(N,2) pairs.\n- **Baseline:** Fleiss' Kappa over BIO token labels.\n- **Evaluation:** Spearman ρ between SJ confidence and instance-level F1.\n- **Best-of-N:** Select sample with highest leave-one-out mean SJ.\n\n## Related Work section covers:\n1. Self-consistency in LLM reasoning (Wang et al. 2023, majority voting)\n2. Test-time scaling (Snell et al. 2024, repeated sampling, verifiers)\n3. Confidence estimation for IE (softmax, attention entropy, calibration)\n4. Agreement metrics (Fleiss' Kappa, Jaccard/Sørensen-Dice)\n\n## Results Summary\n- 3 NER datasets (SciERC, CoNLL-2003, OntoNotes): consistent SJ > FK\n- Cross-model (Qwen3-8B, LLaMA-3.1-8B): SJ advantage holds\n- OOD: ρ degrades 13.4% but remains actionable\n- Temperature analysis: T=0.7 acts as capability filter for RE\n- RE diagnostic: per-type breakdown, capability stratification\n- Best-of-N: +3.9 F1 on SciERC NER\n- Selection curves: SJ > FK > Random across N=1-16\n\n## References (10 total):\n[1] Wang et al. 2023 - Self-consistency (ICLR)\n[2] Brown et al. 2020 - GPT-3 (NeurIPS)\n[3] Snell et al. 2024 - Scaling test-time compute (arXiv)\n[4] Lu et al. 2022 - UIE (ACL)\n[5] Luan et al. 2018 - SciERC (EMNLP)\n[6] Tjong Kim Sang & De Meulder 2003 - CoNLL-2003\n[7] Pradhan et al. 2013 - OntoNotes\n[8] Fleiss 1971 - Fleiss' Kappa\n[9] Jaccard 1912 - Jaccard index\n[10] Hu et al. 2022 - LoRA (ICLR)\n\n## Your Review Task (R2: Novelty + Related Work)\n\nEvaluate:\n1. **Novelty:** How novel is the core idea (using soft Jaccard as self-consistency signal for IE TTS)? Is it a straightforward combination of existing techniques, or does it offer genuine insight? Is the \"structural\" framing justified or is this just \"use a better metric\"?\n2. **Related work coverage:** Are important prior works missing? Specifically consider:\n   - Self-consistency beyond majority voting (e.g., Universal Self-Consistency, USC)\n   - Structured prediction confidence/calibration\n   - IE-specific TTS or multi-sample strategies\n   - Other structural similarity metrics (e.g., tree edit distance, graph matching)\n   - Verifier/reward model approaches for structured outputs\n   - Confidence estimation in NER/RE specifically\n3. **Positioning:** Does the paper adequately position itself relative to prior work? Does it overclaim novelty?\n4. **Contribution significance:** Is this a top-venue contribution? Does the diagnostic analysis (Claim 2) add sufficient depth?\n5. **Writing quality and presentation**\n\nUse a 6-point scale: 1=Strong Reject, 2=Reject, 3=Weak Reject, 4=Weak Accept, 5=Accept, 6=Strong Accept.\n\nProvide:\n- Summary (2-3 sentences)\n- Strengths (bulleted)\n- Weaknesses (bulleted, with severity: minor/moderate/major)\n- Missing related work (specific papers/areas)\n- Questions for authors\n- Score and confidence (1-5)\n","agentId":"ae7321839ca81c909","agentType":"general-purpose","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Review submitted. Here is the summary of my R2 assessment:\n\n**Score: 3 (Weak Reject) | Confidence: 4/5**\n\nKey findings from the Novelty and Related Work perspective:\n\n**Core novelty concern:** The contribution reduces to substituting one existing metric (soft Jaccard) for another (Fleiss' Kappa) as a self-consistency scoring function. The \"structural\" framing overstates what is essentially a metric swap on flat sets of (span, type) tuples, not genuinely structured objects like trees or graphs.\n\n**Critical missing related work:**\n- Universal Self-Consistency (USC, Chen et al., NAACL 2024) -- directly addresses extending self-consistency to non-votable outputs via LLM-as-judge, the most relevant prior work\n- Verifier/reward model TTS approaches (Cobbe et al., Lightman et al.) -- the dominant TTS paradigm, completely absent\n- Sequence log-probability baselines (Kadavath et al., Kuhn et al.) -- the most natural competing selection signal\n- Semantic uncertainty clustering (Kuhn et al., 2023)\n- IE-specific confidence estimation (Culotta & McCallum 2004, etc.)\n\n**Baseline weakness:** Only comparing against Fleiss' Kappa (a weak strawman not designed for structured outputs) cannot establish that soft Jaccard is a strong confidence signal -- it may just confirm FK is a poor one. Missing: log-prob selection, exact-match voting, LLM-as-judge consistency.\n\n**Strongest element:** The RE diagnostic analysis (component decomposition, temperature as capability filter, capability stratification) provides genuine mechanistic insight and is the paper's most publishable contribution.\n\n**Path to acceptance:** Add log-probability and USC-style baselines, expand related work to 30+ references, and either provide theoretical grounding for why soft Jaccard works or demonstrate larger empirical gains. Currently between Findings and Reject."}],"totalDurationMs":149971,"totalTokens":46348,"totalToolUseCount":18,"usage":{"input_tokens":1,"cache_creation_input_tokens":2957,"cache_read_input_tokens":42938,"output_tokens":452,"server_tool_use":{"web_search_requests":0,"web_fetch_requests":0},"service_tier":"standard","cache_creation":{"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":2957},"inference_geo":"","iterations":[{"input_tokens":1,"output_tokens":452,"cache_read_input_tokens":42938,"cache_creation_input_tokens":2957,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":2957,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"type":"message"}],"speed":"standard"},"toolStats":{"readCount":5,"searchCount":3,"bashCount":0,"editFileCount":0,"linesAdded":0,"linesRemoved":0,"otherToolCount":10}}}
