{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-4-6","id":"msg_017K4ogwaKVN6seJAqx2aMsF","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_019MVWx2Rf5hsGxJVjkUAZYB","name":"Agent","input":{"description":"R1 reacts to R2 findings","prompt":"You are Reviewer R1 (Methods + Experiments focus) in a DREAM review roundtable. You gave this paper a 4 (Weak Accept). Now you've seen R2's (Novelty + Related Work) review. React to R2's points and state whether they change your assessment.\n\n## Your Original R1 Review Summary\n**Score: 4 (Weak Accept)**\nKey strengths: Well-motivated metric design, comprehensive 11-experiment coverage, strong statistical significance (p<10^-27), honest RE negative result treatment, practical best-of-N validation.\nKey weaknesses: (Major) Possible SJ formula discrepancy between paper and implementation (paper uses symmetric max-match, implementation may use Hungarian matching); (Major) Evaluation circularity concern (correlating SJ with greedy F1 rather than sample F1); (Major) Inconsistent N across Table 1 (SciERC N=16 vs others N=8, confounding comparison); (Moderate) Only 1 RE dataset; (Moderate) No confidence intervals; (Moderate) Limited baselines beyond FK.\n\n## R2's Review Summary (Novelty + Related Work)\n**Score: 3 (Weak Reject)**\nKey points:\n1. **Core novelty is thin:** The contribution reduces to substituting soft Jaccard for Fleiss' Kappa as a self-consistency scoring function. The \"structural\" framing overstates what is essentially a metric swap on flat sets.\n2. **Critical missing related work:**\n   - Universal Self-Consistency (USC, Chen et al., NAACL 2024) -- extends self-consistency to non-votable outputs via LLM-as-judge, the most relevant prior work\n   - Verifier/reward model TTS approaches (Cobbe et al., Lightman et al.)\n   - Sequence log-probability baselines (Kadavath et al., Kuhn et al.)\n   - Semantic uncertainty clustering (Kuhn et al. 2023)\n   - IE-specific confidence estimation\n3. **FK is a strawman baseline** not designed for structured outputs -- beating it doesn't prove SJ is strong.\n4. **Only 10 references** is far too few for a top venue.\n5. **Strongest element:** The RE diagnostic analysis provides genuine mechanistic insight.\n\n## Your Task\n1. Do you agree that the novelty concern is serious? Does the \"metric swap\" characterization fairly describe the contribution?\n2. Do you agree that the missing baselines (especially log-probability) undermine the experimental story?\n3. Does R2's point about USC (Universal Self-Consistency) change your view? Is USC a direct competitor that should have been compared?\n4. Would you adjust your score based on R2's feedback? State your updated score if any.\n\nKeep your response under 400 words.\n"},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":1,"cache_creation_input_tokens":6749,"cache_read_input_tokens":58941,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":6749},"output_tokens":8,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"2168b128-bc29-48d5-839e-470b564c8edf","uuid":"2bea2075-bfa7-42cd-b876-4849c905fe6a"}
