{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"type":"text","text":"You are Reviewer R2 (Novelty + Related Work focus) in a DREAM review roundtable. You gave this paper a 3 (Weak Reject). Now you've seen R1's (Methods + Experiments) review. React to R1's points and state whether they change your assessment.\n\n## Your Original R2 Review Summary\n**Score: 3 (Weak Reject)**\nKey points: Core novelty is thin (metric swap from FK to SJ). Missing critical related work (USC, verifiers, log-prob baselines, semantic uncertainty). FK is a strawman. Only 10 references. RE diagnostic analysis is the strongest contribution.\n\n## R1's Review Summary (Methods + Experiments)\n**Score: 4 (Weak Accept)**\nKey points:\n1. **(Major) SJ formula discrepancy:** Paper Equation 1 uses symmetric max-match with denominator |y_a|+|y_b|, but implementation may use Hungarian matching within type groups with max(|ga|,|gb|) denominator per group. These are different algorithms.\n2. **(Major) Evaluation protocol circularity:** The confidence score is correlated with greedy (T=0) F1, but best-of-N selection operates on T=0.7 samples. Why is greedy F1 the right proxy?\n3. **(Major) Inconsistent N across Table 1:** SciERC uses N=16, CoNLL and OntoNotes use N=8. Table 2 shows N affects rho by +0.065, so SciERC's rho_SJ=.448 at N=8 would be ~.383, the lowest of three datasets -- completely changes the headline comparison.\n4. **(Moderate) Only 1 RE dataset, rho ranges in Table 7 instead of point estimates.**\n5. **(Moderate) No confidence intervals for individual rho estimates.**\n6. **(Moderate) Limited baselines -- FK may be a strawman (R1 independently agrees with you).**\n7. Positives: thorough experiments (11/11 planned covered), strong p-values, insightful RE diagnostic.\n\n## Your Task\n1. Does R1's formula discrepancy concern affect your novelty assessment? (If the paper formula differs from implementation, this compounds the \"thin contribution\" concern.)\n2. Does the Table 1 N inconsistency (SciERC inflated by N=16) change how you evaluate the claims?\n3. Does R1's evaluation circularity point (correlating with greedy F1 vs sample F1) raise new concerns for you?\n4. Does the experimental comprehensiveness that R1 highlights (11 experiments, thorough ablations) partially offset your novelty concern?\n5. Would you adjust your score? State updated score if any.\n\nKeep your response under 400 words.\n"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":"toolu_017aoz1uW276jBm7tjSbXYjt","session_id":"2168b128-bc29-48d5-839e-470b564c8edf","uuid":"a2b37faf-d1e8-4f1e-9bf1-f2aed9bd2dd4","timestamp":"2026-05-14T10:12:10.115Z"}
