{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-4-6","id":"msg_01A8C6BurnrZoRPAbSSUsj4q","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_019pjCVpQH2hENd2iyqzdZEc","name":"mcp__agent-ml-research__submit_dream_review_report","input":{"report":"# Dream Review Report\n\n**Paper:** Structural Self-Consistency Enables Test-Time Scaling for Entity-Level Information Extraction\n**Review Mode:** Full (2 Reviewers, 4-Step Roundtable)\n**Date:** 2026-05-14\n\n---\n\n## Overall Assessment\n\n**Score: 3 / 6 (Weak Reject)**\n\nBoth reviewers converged on Weak Reject after cross-discussion (R1 initially at 4, revised to 3 after seeing R2's novelty/positioning concerns; R2 held at 3 throughout). The paper addresses a relevant problem—test-time scaling for structured prediction—and provides thorough experiments with honest treatment of negative results. However, it suffers from (1) thin novelty that reduces to a metric substitution without adequate positioning against the most relevant prior work, (2) missing critical baselines that prevent assessing whether the gains come from the specific metric design or from any reasonable scoring function, and (3) methodological issues including inconsistent experimental conditions across the main comparison table and a possible formula discrepancy.\n\n---\n\n## Consensus Strengths\n\n- **Thorough experimental coverage.** All 11 planned experiments are present in the paper, spanning 3 NER datasets (SciERC, CoNLL-2003, OntoNotes), 2 model families (Qwen3-8B, LLaMA-3.1-8B), OOD settings, temperature analysis, capability stratification, per-relation-type decomposition, and N-scaling curves. This is commendable breadth for a primarily analytical contribution.\n\n- **Strong statistical significance.** All SJ vs FK comparisons for NER report p < 10^{-27}, with large test sets (CoNLL 3,453; OntoNotes 8,262) providing high power.\n\n- **Insightful RE diagnostic analysis (strongest element).** The per-relation-type breakdown (Table 7: r=0.72 with frequency, r=-0.65 with span distance), capability stratification (Table 8: inverted-U pattern with RE matching NER-level rho in the 0.6-0.9 bucket), and temperature-as-capability-filter analysis (Table 5) together construct a mechanistic understanding of when and why structural consistency degrades. The distinction between \"uncertainty\" and \"inability\" is a genuine conceptual contribution that transcends this specific metric.\n\n- **Practical validation.** Best-of-N selection converts correlation into concrete gains (+3.9 F1, 26.9% headroom captured), and selection curves (Table 10) show monotonic SJ advantage across all N values.\n\n- **Honest negative result treatment.** The RE analysis does not hide failure but uses it constructively, providing diagnostic guidance for practitioners.\n\n---\n\n## Consensus Weaknesses\n\n### Major Issues\n\n**1. Missing critical related work and baselines (both reviewers, highest priority).**\nThe paper cites only 10 references—far below the ~30-40 expected at a top venue. Critical omissions include:\n- **Universal Self-Consistency (USC, Chen et al., NAACL 2024):** Directly addresses extending self-consistency to non-votable structured outputs via LLM-as-judge. This is the most relevant prior work and must be cited, discussed, and ideally compared against.\n- **Verifier/reward model TTS** (Cobbe et al., Lightman et al.): The dominant TTS paradigm, entirely absent.\n- **Sequence log-probability baselines** (Kadavath et al., Kuhn et al.): The most natural competing selection signal, requiring no additional computation.\n- **Semantic uncertainty** (Kuhn et al., 2023): Clustering-based uncertainty for free-form LLM outputs.\n- **IE-specific confidence estimation** (Culotta & McCallum 2004, etc.).\n\nWithout log-probability, USC, or semantic uncertainty baselines, it is impossible to determine whether SJ's gains over FK reflect the merit of the specific metric or simply the weakness of FK as a baseline. FK (BIO token-level agreement) is not designed for structured set outputs and may be a strawman. Both reviewers independently flagged this.\n\n**2. Thin novelty under \"structural\" framing (R2, endorsed by R1).**\nThe contribution reduces to substituting one existing metric (soft Jaccard) for another (Fleiss' Kappa) as a self-consistency scoring function. The \"structural\" framing overstates the contribution: SJ operates on flat sets of (span, type) tuples and does not model inter-entity dependencies, schema constraints, or graph structure. A more honest framing would be \"set-aware self-consistency scoring.\" The diagnostic analysis (Claim 2) provides genuine depth but alone is insufficient to cross the novelty threshold without either (a) theoretical grounding for why SJ works, or (b) stronger empirical differentiation from natural baselines.\n\n**3. Inconsistent N across Table 1 (R1, endorsed by R2).**\nThe headline cross-dataset comparison (Table 1) uses N=16 for SciERC but N=8 for CoNLL and OntoNotes. Table 2 shows N=8→16 improves ρ_SJ by +0.065 on SciERC. At N=8, SciERC's ρ_SJ would be ~0.383—the *lowest* of three datasets, not the highest as Table 1 presents. This confound undermines the cross-dataset generalization narrative. The main comparison table must use consistent N across all datasets.\n\n**4. Possible SJ formula discrepancy (R1).**\nEquation 1 defines SJ via symmetric max-match with denominator |y_a|+|y_b|, which permits many-to-one matching. The implementation may use Hungarian (optimal one-to-one) matching within type groups with max(|g_a|,|g_b|) denominator per group. These are different algorithms that can produce different rankings. The paper does not mention type-group matching or one-to-one constraints. Readers attempting to reproduce from the paper formula would get different results.\n\n### Moderate Issues\n\n**5. Evaluation protocol circularity (R1, endorsed by R2).**\nThe confidence score c(x) (computed from T=0.7 samples) is correlated against greedy (T=0) F1 as the evaluation metric, but best-of-N selection operates on the T=0.7 samples. The paper does not justify why greedy F1 is the right proxy for selection quality among stochastic samples. A more direct evaluation would correlate each sample's SJ score with that sample's own F1.\n\n**6. Single RE dataset (R1).**\nWhile 3 NER datasets are tested, all RE analysis relies on SciERC (397 instances with relations, 7 types). The diagnostic claims about compound error propagation are plausible but dataset-specific. A second RE dataset (TACRED, DocRED) would substantially strengthen Claim 2.\n\n**7. No confidence intervals for ρ (R1).**\nBootstrap CIs for individual ρ values are absent. Some capability-stratified analyses have small n (42-85 instances per bucket), where ρ estimation is noisy.\n\n**8. Table 7 reports ρ as ranges (R1).**\nPer-relation-type ρ_SJ values are given as ranges (e.g., \"0.36-0.40\") rather than point estimates, which is unusual and imprecise. The source of these ranges is not explained (multiple seeds? bootstrap? conditions?).\n\n### Minor Issues\n\n**9. No RE best-of-N results.** The adaptive compute demo and selection curves are NER-only. Even a negative result for RE would complete the story.\n\n**10. Temperature table headroom mismatch.** Table 5 reports NER T=0.7 headroom as 0.055 while Table 2 reports N=8 headroom as 0.108—the relationship between these conditions should be clarified.\n\n---\n\n## Questions for Authors\n\n1. Can you reconcile Equation 1 (symmetric max-match, |y_a|+|y_b| denominator) with the actual implementation? Which version produced the reported results?\n2. For Table 1, can you provide all three datasets at N=8 for an apples-to-apples comparison?\n3. How does SJ compare to sequence log-probability as a selection signal? This requires no additional inference and is the most natural baseline.\n4. Are you aware of Universal Self-Consistency (Chen et al., NAACL 2024)? How does your approach compare in cost and effectiveness?\n5. What explains the ρ ranges (rather than point estimates) in Table 7?\n6. Why is confidence correlated against greedy F1 rather than each sample's own F1?\n\n---\n\n## Reviewer Score Summary\n\n| Reviewer | Focus | Initial Score | Post-Discussion Score | Confidence |\n|----------|-------|---------------|----------------------|------------|\n| R1 | Methods + Experiments | 4 (Weak Accept) | 3 (Weak Reject) | 4/5 |\n| R2 | Novelty + Related Work | 3 (Weak Reject) | 3 (Weak Reject) | 4/5 |\n| **Consensus** | | | **3 (Weak Reject)** | |\n\n---\n\n## Path to Acceptance\n\nThe paper has a viable core contribution that could reach acceptance with targeted revisions:\n\n1. **Add baselines (critical):** Compare SJ against sequence log-probability selection, exact-match voting rate, and ideally USC or semantic uncertainty. If SJ outperforms log-prob, the metric design argument becomes compelling.\n2. **Fix Table 1 (critical):** Report all datasets at consistent N (either all N=8 or all N=16).\n3. **Expand related work (critical):** Cite and discuss USC, verifier-based TTS, semantic uncertainty, and IE-specific confidence literature. Target 25-30+ references.\n4. **Clarify SJ formula (critical):** Align the paper's equation with the actual implementation, including type-group matching and denominator choice.\n5. **Add RE best-of-N results and a second RE dataset (important).**\n6. **Add bootstrap CIs for ρ (important).**\n7. **Reframe novelty honestly:** \"Set-aware self-consistency\" rather than \"structural.\" The diagnostic framework is the paper's strongest differentiator—foreground it.\n\nWith these revisions, the thorough experimental coverage and insightful diagnostic analysis could support a Weak Accept to Accept outcome at a top venue.\n\n---\n\n## Experiment Coverage Assessment\n\nAll 11 planned experiments are reflected in the paper:\n| # | Experiment | Status | Table(s) |\n|---|-----------|--------|----------|\n| 1 | NER N=16 SciERC | Covered | Table 2 |\n| 2 | NER CoNLL-2003 | Covered | Table 1 |\n| 3 | NER OntoNotes | Covered | Table 1 |\n| 4 | RE diagnostic per-type | Covered | Table 7 |\n| 5 | Temperature sensitivity | Covered | Table 5 |\n| 6 | Adaptive compute best-of-N | Covered | Table 9 |\n| 7 | Cross-model LLaMA | Covered | Table 3 |\n| 8 | RE N=16 confirmation | Covered | Table 6 |\n| 9 | Capability stratification | Covered | Table 8 |\n| 10 | Selection curves | Covered | Table 10 |\n| 11 | OOD robustness | Covered | Table 4 |\n\nCoverage: **11/11 (100%)**. No planned experiments are missing from the paper.\n","project":"struct_self_consist_ie","tier":"best","dream_id":"20260514T093336","overall_score":"weak reject","review_visible":true},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":3,"cache_creation_input_tokens":3257,"cache_read_input_tokens":65690,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":3257},"output_tokens":1,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"2168b128-bc29-48d5-839e-470b564c8edf","uuid":"b2d3c51a-88ae-48b3-af4b-76d739beacd4"}
